**Whatever this listing shows, `list_plans` is the authority.** Call it before
any `set_draft`. The listing is for choosing a plan, never for acting on one,
and it can be both incomplete and stale:

- The listing is a **snapshot taken at build time**. Plans registered since the
  build, plans authored during this session, and plans supplied through the
  older `bluesky.plan_module` configuration are all absent from it.
- The listing is **metadata-level**. It reads each plan file's declared
  metadata and nothing more, while the bridge's loader also rejects a plan
  whose `build_plan` is missing or not callable, whose `PARAMS` are malformed,
  or that declares it moves the machine without naming a movable channel. A
  plan listed here may therefore be quarantined at load and never appear in
  `list_plans` at all.
- The deployed bridge may run a **pinned image** whose plan files differ from
  the ones the build host saw.

---

## Reuse first; author only when nothing fits

1. **Look for a registered plan.** Call `list_plans` and read what comes back.
   If a plan covers the request — even approximately, since most plans are
   parameterised — that is the path.
   → Use the **`operating-bluesky-plans`** skill. It owns staging, queuing,
   starting, and watching the run.
2. **Author only when nothing fits.** A new plan is warranted when no
   registered plan expresses the measurement at all, not when an existing one
   would need unfamiliar arguments.
   → Use the **`writing-bluesky-plans`** skill. It owns the plan-file format,
   validation, and how a newly written plan becomes runnable.

A plan written this session is session-tier and inert until it validates, so
authoring is the longer road. Take it deliberately, not by default.

---

## Checking what a plan actually does

When the question is what a specific parameter means, which channels a plan
touches, or whether it really matches what was asked for, **read the plan**:
`get_plan_source(name)` returns the file's own text. It is a read — it starts
nothing.

Prefer it to paraphrasing a one-line catalog description. Those descriptions
are written to help someone pick a plan, not to settle what an argument does,
and guessing at a parameter's meaning is how the wrong value reaches the
machine. If the response comes back `truncated`, ask again with a larger
`max_chars`, up to the 200000 ceiling, rather than describing an ending you
have not read. At that ceiling the rest of the file is simply not retrievable
through this tool, and must not be described at all.
